        "---\n",
        "title: \"Example: Attention Scores from Scratch\"\n",
        "date: \"2026-04-10\"\n",
        "categories: [attention, example]\n",
        "draft: true\n",
        "jupyter: mechinterp\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a demo post showing what Quarto can do — LaTeX, code, and plots in one place.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The attention formula\n",
        "\n",
        "Given queries $Q \\in \\mathbb{R}^{T \\times d_k}$ and keys $K \\in \\mathbb{R}^{T \\times d_k}$, the attention pattern is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$A = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^\\top}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The $\\sqrt{d_k}$ scaling prevents the dot products from growing large when $d_k$ is big, which would push softmax into saturation (near-zero gradients).\n",
        "\n",
        "For a single query vector $q$ and key matrix $K$, the score for position $j$ is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$a_j = \\frac{\\exp(q \\cdot k_j / \\sqrt{d_k})}{\\sum_{i} \\exp(q \\cdot k_i / \\sqrt{d_k})}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## Computing it by hand\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's say $d_k = 2$ and we have 3 tokens with:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$Q = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad K = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The raw scores $QK^\\top / \\sqrt{2}$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Row 3 (token 3) attends most strongly to itself — its query $(1,1)$ aligns best with key $(1,1)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## In code"
      ],
      "id": "cdca1395"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import torch\n",
        "import torch.nn.functional as F\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "Q = torch.tensor([[1., 0.], [0., 1.], [1., 1.]])\n",
        "K = torch.tensor([[1., 0.], [0., 1.], [1., 1.]])\n",
        "\n",
        "d_k = Q.shape[-1]\n",
        "scores = Q @ K.T / d_k**0.5\n",
        "attn = F.softmax(scores, dim=-1)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Attention pattern:\")\n",
        "print(attn.round(decimals=3))"
      ],
      "id": "aa2391ec",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Visualizing the pattern"
      ],
      "id": "76f3db80"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(4, 3))\n",
        "im = ax.imshow(attn.numpy(), cmap=\"Blues\", vmin=0, vmax=1)\n",
        "ax.set_xticks([0,1,2]); ax.set_xticklabels([\"tok 1\",\"tok 2\",\"tok 3\"])\n",
        "ax.set_yticks([0,1,2]); ax.set_yticklabels([\"tok 1\",\"tok 2\",\"tok 3\"])\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel(\"Key\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"Query\")\n",
        "ax.set_title(\"Attention pattern\")\n",
        "plt.colorbar(im, ax=ax)\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "370d0165",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The diagonal dominance means each token attends most to itself — makes sense since $Q = K$ here. In a real transformer the weights learned to make $Q \\neq K$, which is where interesting structure emerges."
      ],
